  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined over $A$ and $B$ is *functional* (or a *partial function*) if, for all $a\\in A$ and ${b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\in B,$ we have \n\n$$\n(a,{b}_{1})\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(a,{b}_{2})\\in R\\,\u21d2\\,{b}_{1}={b}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the first components of two ordered pairs are equal, then the second components must also be equal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- $R$ is *not* functional. Notice that $1={1}^{2}$ and $1=(-1)^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains pairs $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ with the same first component but different second components.\n\n- $S$ is functional. Indeed, $a{b}_{1}^{3}=1$ and $a{b}_{2}^{3}=1$ is equivalent to ${b}_{1}={b}_{2}$ over ${R}^{+}.$ So, when the first components of two pairs are equal, then the second components are also equal.\n\n- $T$ is *not* functional. Notice that $2\u22232(3)$ and $3\u22232(3).$ So, $T$ contains pairs $(3,2)$ and $(3,3)$ with the same first component but different second components.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\"",
